// The stationary pressure tank, and the frame it is welded out of.
//
// One family of `tfs/assets/sprites.ron`'s roster. Every .ron in
// this directory is baked, in FILE-NAME order and then expansion order,
// so the atlas layout is a function of the manifest and not of the
// filesystem. Two files claiming one sprite name is a bake error.
//
// tg's tank is a SMOOTHED object: `smoothing_flags = SMOOTH_BITMASK`
// with `smoothing_groups = SMOOTH_GROUP_GAS_TANK` (tank.dm:25-27), and
// its sheet carries all 47 junction states a bank of tanks can stand
// in. Nothing in this engine resolves a junction bitmask to a sprite
// name, so only the ISOLATED state is baked — `canister-0`, the tank
// with no neighbour on any side, which is what a single vessel with
// walking room around it looks like in tg too. The other 46 are the
// picture of a merged bank, and the merger that would justify them is
// deferred with it (see `AtmosDevice::volume_l`).
//
// COLOUR. Both sheets are greyscale art: tg tints them through GAGS
// (`greyscale_config = /datum/greyscale_config/stationary_canister`,
// tank.dm:30-32), and both json configs are a single `icon_state`
// layer at `color_ids: [1]` — one flat multiply and nothing else. The
// colour is NOT a property of the gas inside; it is the PLATING
// MATERIAL's, taken from `custom_materials` through `MATERIAL_GREYSCALE`
// (tank.dm:17-18, atom_materials.dm:255-294). tg's default plating is
// iron, `/datum/material/iron` `color = "#B6BEC2"` (basemats.dm:2-5),
// which is the (182, 190, 194) below. `Tinted` keeps the pixels grey
// and hands that colour to the renderer, so the day a tank remembers
// what it was plated with, one object can paint itself without a
// second bake.
//
// The knobs, the frame and the plated frame are baked RAW, because tg
// draws them raw: neither greyscale config mentions `knob`, `frame` or
// `plated_frame`, and `knob_overlays` is built straight off the .dmi
// (tank.dm:77-80). tg's ten crack states are skipped entirely — they
// belong to the `crackable` element, which is deferred, and they are
// pre-coloured translucent green rather than greyscale anyway.
(
    sheets: [
        ("tank", "obj/pipes_n_cables/stationary_canisters.dmi"),
        ("tank_misc", "obj/pipes_n_cables/stationary_canisters_misc.dmi"),
    ],
    sprites: [
        // The window in its side, both of tg's layers in one cell.
        //
        // tg stacks three things here: a flat `window-bg` backing on
        // the shell (tank.dm:321), the gas visuals suspended over it
        // and alpha-masked to it (tank.dm:372-374), and `window-fg` —
        // the glass — over those (tank.dm:358-361). We have no per-gas
        // visuals, so the middle layer is gone and the other two are
        // one picture.
        //
        // They MUST be baked together. Each alone is what
        // `no_sprite_is_a_bare_colour_mask` exists to catch: the
        // backing is 87 pixels of pure white (it is a mask, literally),
        // and the glass is 44 pixels 91% of one grey. Composited they
        // are a lit pane with a highlight on it, which is what tg
        // actually shows on an empty tank. `paint: (255, 255, 255)` is
        // the identity multiply — the real colour is `Tinted`'s, handed
        // to the renderer rather than baked in, so the tank's plating
        // material can paint it later.
        ("stationary_tank_window", Tinted(DmiBlend(
            dmi: "@tank_misc",
            layers: [
                ("window-bg", Over, true),
                ("window-fg", Over, true),
            ],
            dir: 0,
            paint: (255, 255, 255),
        ), (182, 190, 194))),
    ],
    groups: [
        // The shell. tg's `post_init_icon_state = "canister-0"`
        // (tank.dm:9) is the state a tank wears before smoothing runs,
        // and here it is the only one it ever wears.
        (sheet: "tank", tint: Some((182, 190, 194)), sprites: [
            Sprite("stationary_tank", "canister-0", s),
        ]),

        // One knob per OPEN PORT, dir-rotated — tg's `knob_overlays`
        // (tank.dm:77-80, 322-326). The only four-direction state in
        // either sheet, and the only art in the tank that is a reading
        // rather than decoration: a knob is where a pipe may be welded
        // on, so a glance says which sides of a vessel are live.
        //
        // ...and the two frame stages, `frame` and `plated_frame`
        // (tank.dm:561-562, 593-599), which are what stands on the deck
        // between four sheets of metal and a sealed tank.
        (sheet: "tank_misc", sprites: [
            Directions("stationary_tank_knob", "knob"),
            Sprite("stationary_tank_frame", "frame", s),
            Sprite("stationary_tank_plated", "plated_frame", s),
        ]),
    ],
)
